7.1  What a container really is

TL;DR
  - A container is a group of ordinary processes on your kernel. Nothing is
    emulated: uname -r inside shows the host's kernel.
  - Three kernel features make them feel apart: namespaces (what they see),
    cgroups (what they may use) and capabilities and seccomp (what they may do).
    An image gives them their own files.
  - So containers start in milliseconds and cost little, but they all depend on
    the host's kernel.

      docker run --rm -it alpine sh     # then: ps, hostname, uname -r
      ps -ef --forest                   # on the host: the same processes

On your machine
  Here      no container or virtual machine detected
  Isolated  none: the host's namespaces (pid, user, uts, ipc, cgroup, time)
  Can run   VMs with KVM (/dev/kvm) · containers with Docker
  Overlays  1 overlay mount: container layers, usually

Pitfalls
  - Your program as PID 1 doesn't stop on SIGTERM unless it handles it:
    docker stop waits 10 seconds, then kills it. --init adds a tiny init.
  - A container can't run a different kernel: Linux images only, and kernel
    modules or sysctls come from the host.
  - On a Mac or Windows, Docker and Podman run Linux in a virtual machine, and
    the containers inside it.
  - Anything written inside a container is gone when it's removed, unless it's
    in a volume (7.3).

Deep dive

  ▾ What does docker run actually do?

    The engine unpacks the image's layers, stacks them as the root filesystem,
    then asks a runtime (runc or crun) to start the process: it creates new
    namespaces, puts the process in a cgroup with its limits, drops
    capabilities, applies a seccomp filter of allowed system calls, and calls
    exec. From then on it's just a process the kernel schedules like any other.

  ▾ Container or virtual machine?

    A VM runs its own kernel on emulated hardware: stronger isolation, any
    operating system, more memory and a slower start. A container shares the
    host's kernel: light and fast, but a kernel flaw affects them all. Clouds
    often combine them: containers inside small VMs (7.4).

Related
  Commands  docker, podman, uname
  Paths     /sys/fs/cgroup/, /proc/self/ns/
  See also  idk 7.2 Namespaces: a private view of the system
  See also  idk 16.4 cgroups
  See also  idk 7.4 Virtual machines and hypervisors
